    "lawCode": "Amd §7409, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8522\n\nSPONSOR: HELMING\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nmandatory continuing education requirements for certified public\naccountants\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to reduce the three-year exemption of manda-\ntory continuing education for newly licensed Certified Public Account-\nants and public accountants to a one-year exemption.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 - Amends paragraph 1(b) of Section 7409 of the Education Law\nto reduce the three-year exemption of mandatory continuing education for\nnewly licensed Certified Public Accountants and public accountants to a\none-year exemption.\n\nSection 2 - Sets an effective date of January 1, 2020.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrently, Certified Public Accountants and public accountants are\nexempt from the mandatory continuing education requirement for the\ntriennial registration period during which they are first licensed by\nthe department.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMandating Continuing Professional Education (CPE) for all CPAs after the\nfirst year of licensure in New York is in the public's best interest. As\nthe pace of technology, proliferation of regulations and increasing\nspecialization accelerates; one of the greatest challenges for all CPAs\nis the attainment, maintenance and advancement of professional compe-\ntence. To meet this challenge and to maintain public confidence it is\ncritical that all CPAs are and remain current with the standards, know-\nledge, skills, and abilities in all areas in which they provide\nservices. It is not enough that an individual is successful in complet-\ning an accountancy education and passing an exam.  In today's complex\nand complicated world, all CPAs need to demonstrate that they are taking\nsubstantive steps to maintain licensure. Unprofessional or unethical\npractice of accountancy causes harm to the public. After the first year\nof licensure, mandatory CPEs for all CPAs will further increase public\nprotection in the accountancy profession.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nJanuary 1, 2020\n",
